-- First listening-passage content round: 18 passages (3 per CEFR
-- level), 54 linked mcq questions. Closes the audit-flagged gap where
-- every listening item was a single decontextualized sentence.
-- passages.audio_url is left NULL here (falls back to browser TTS
-- reading passages.body aloud) -- real audio is generated afterward
-- via the admin panel's 'Generate passage audio' button.

WITH lp_passage_0 AS (
    VALUES ('Tom Calls In Sick', 'Tom calls his manager, Mrs. Green, in the morning. He says, ''Hello, this is Tom. I am sick today. I have a headache.'' Mrs. Green says, ''I am sorry to hear that. Please rest at home.'' Tom says, ''Thank you. I will come back to work tomorrow.'' Mrs. Green says, ''Okay, feel better soon.''', 'listening', 'A1', 'call')
  RETURNING id
)

WITH lp_passage_1 AS (
    VALUES ('New Report Deadline', 'Every Monday, the team has a meeting at ten o''clock. Today, the manager says, ''The report is not for Monday now. The report is for Friday.'' One worker asks, ''Can I send it by email?'' The manager says, ''Yes. Please send it to my email before five o''clock.''', 'listening', 'A1', 'meeting')
  RETURNING id
)

WITH lp_passage_2 AS (
    VALUES ('Returning a Jacket', 'A customer walks into the shop with a black jacket. She says to the worker, ''I bought this jacket yesterday, but it is too small. Can I return it?'' The worker says, ''Yes, do you have the receipt?'' The customer says, ''Yes, here it is.'' The worker says, ''Thank you. I will give you your money back now.''', 'listening', 'A1', 'customer_service')
  RETURNING id
)

WITH lp_passage_3 AS (
    VALUES ('The Wrong Delivery', 'Tom works at a small office. This morning, a delivery driver arrives with three boxes of paper. Tom checks the order and sees a problem. He says, "We ordered two boxes of pens, not paper." The driver looks at his sheet and says, "Sorry, this delivery is for the office next door." Tom calls the driver''s company. A woman answers and says, "Don''t worry, we will collect the paper this afternoon and bring your pens tomorrow morning." Tom thanks her and puts the boxes near the front door.', 'listening', 'A2', 'customer_service')
  RETURNING id
)

WITH lp_passage_4 AS (
    VALUES ('Changing the Meeting Time', 'Sophie calls her colleague Marc to change their meeting. She says, "Hi Marc, can we move our meeting from Tuesday to Wednesday? I have a training course on Tuesday morning." Marc checks his calendar and says, "Wednesday is fine, but not before eleven o''clock. I have a call with a client at ten." Sophie suggests, "Let''s meet at two in the afternoon, in the small meeting room on the third floor." Marc agrees and says he will send a new invitation before the end of the day.', 'listening', 'A2', 'meeting')
  RETURNING id
)

WITH lp_passage_5 AS (
    VALUES ('Office Announcement', 'The office manager makes an announcement at the start of the day. She says, "Good morning everyone. First, the coffee machine on the second floor is broken, so please use the one in the kitchen. Second, the car park will close for cleaning tomorrow from nine until midday. Please park on the street instead. Finally, don''t forget the team lunch on Friday at one o''clock in the café downstairs." Everyone listens carefully and a few people write notes about the car park.', 'listening', 'A2', 'general')
  RETURNING id
)

WITH lp_passage_6 AS (
    VALUES ('A Late Shipment', 'A warehouse manager calls a supplier about a delayed delivery. She says, ''We were expecting the shipment on Monday, but it still hasn''t arrived, and our production line is waiting for those parts.'' The supplier apologises and explains that a problem at their loading dock delayed the order by two days. He offers to send the shipment by express courier at no extra cost, and promises it will arrive by Thursday morning at the latest. The manager thanks him but asks him to email a written confirmation of the new delivery date, so she can update her production schedule and inform her team before the end of the day.', 'listening', 'B1', 'call')
  RETURNING id
)

WITH lp_passage_7 AS (
    VALUES ('Rescheduling the Budget Meeting', 'During a Monday morning meeting, the finance team discusses next quarter''s budget. The team leader announces that the meeting originally planned for Friday afternoon has been moved to Thursday morning instead, because two key managers will be travelling to a conference on Friday. She explains that the main goal of the new meeting will be to decide how much to allocate to the marketing campaign that has been proposed for the autumn launch. One colleague mentions that she has been reviewing last year''s figures all week and has found that marketing spending was underused by almost fifteen percent. The team leader asks her to present these findings at the rescheduled meeting.', 'listening', 'B1', 'meeting')
  RETURNING id
)

WITH lp_passage_8 AS (
    VALUES ('A Complaint About a Faulty Printer', 'A customer calls a company''s support line to complain about a printer she bought two weeks ago. She explains that it worked fine for the first few days, but it has been jamming constantly since last weekend, and she has already lost several important documents because of it. The support agent listens carefully and apologises for the inconvenience. He checks her order number and confirms that the printer is still under warranty, so she will not have to pay for a repair or a replacement. He offers her a choice: she can send the printer back for a full refund, or the company can send a replacement unit within three working days. She decides to ask for the replacement, since she needs a working printer for her home office.', 'listening', 'B1', 'customer_service')
  RETURNING id
)

WITH lp_passage_9 AS (
    VALUES ('Choosing Between Two Candidates', 'Following the final interview round, the hiring manager called her colleague on the panel to compare notes on the two finalists. She admitted that the first candidate''s technical skills were clearly stronger, but confessed she''d left the interview feeling slightly uneasy about how dismissively he''d spoken about a previous team''s process failures. Her colleague pointed out that the second candidate, while less experienced technically, had asked several thoughtful questions about how the team collaborates under pressure, which he took as a good sign. The hiring manager agreed, adding that technical gaps could be closed through training, but attitude was harder to change. After a short pause, she said she''d rather bring on someone slightly underqualified who fit well than risk a repeat of the friction they''d had with a difficult hire two years earlier. They agreed to recommend the second candidate to leadership, with a note suggesting additional technical mentoring during the first few months.', 'listening', 'B2', 'general')
  RETURNING id
)

WITH lp_passage_10 AS (
    VALUES ('Negotiating the Contract Renewal', 'During the supplier negotiation, Tom opened by proposing a two-year contract at current rates, hoping to lock in stability for both sides. The supplier''s representative pushed back, insisting that rising material costs meant a five percent increase was non-negotiable if the contract were to run beyond twelve months. Tom countered by suggesting a one-year term with a built-in review clause, allowing prices to be revisited once costs became clearer. There was a long silence before the representative admitted that a shorter term with flexibility actually suited her company too, since she''d been hesitant to commit to two years given how unpredictable the market currently was. They ultimately agreed on a twelve-month contract with a quarterly pricing review, and Tom left the meeting relieved that he hadn''t given in to the initial demand for a blanket increase.', 'listening', 'B2', 'negotiation')
  RETURNING id
)

WITH lp_passage_11 AS (
    VALUES ('Feedback After the Product Launch', 'After the product launch, Elena called James into her office to discuss how the project had gone. She began by praising the creativity of his marketing campaign, noting that engagement numbers had exceeded expectations. However, she then mentioned, somewhat carefully, that the launch timeline had slipped by nearly two weeks, which had put pressure on the sales team to catch up before quarter-end. James explained that a last-minute change in design requirements had forced the delay, though he admitted he probably should have flagged the risk earlier instead of trying to absorb it quietly. Elena nodded and said she valued his honesty, adding that next time she''d rather hear about problems early, even if it meant an uncomfortable conversation, than discover them once it was too late to adjust. She ended by saying she still wanted him leading the next launch.', 'listening', 'B2', 'meeting')
  RETURNING id
)

WITH lp_passage_12 AS (
    VALUES ('Renegotiating the Supplier Contract', 'During a quarterly review call, a procurement manager named Elena raised the subject of the annual contract renewal with a long-standing supplier. She began by acknowledging that the partnership had been valuable over the past three years, praising the supplier''s reliability during the previous winter''s shortages. However, she then noted that market conditions had shifted considerably, and that two competing vendors had recently submitted quotes that were, in her words, ''meaningfully more attractive.'' She stopped short of naming any figures, instead suggesting that if the current terms could not be revisited, procurement would be obliged to open the contract to broader tender when it expired in September. The supplier''s representative pushed back gently, arguing that the quality guarantees embedded in the existing agreement justified the premium, and proposed a compromise: a modest reduction in the per-unit rate in exchange for extending the contract term to five years. Elena thanked him for the flexibility but said she would need to consult her director before committing to anything beyond the current cycle, and the call closed with both parties agreeing to reconvene the following week.', 'listening', 'C1', 'negotiation')
  RETURNING id
)

WITH lp_passage_13 AS (
    VALUES ('Briefing the Team on the Department Merger', 'At a hastily convened team meeting, a department head named Marcus informed his staff that the customer support and technical support functions would be merged into a single unit by the end of the quarter, as part of a wider efficiency drive across the company. He emphasised that the decision had been made at a senior level and that his role was simply to ensure the transition went smoothly. When asked directly whether this would mean job losses, Marcus said only that ''headcount decisions are still being finalised'' and that he ''couldn''t get ahead of a process that wasn''t yet complete,'' though he added that anyone with concerns about their role was welcome to speak with him privately. He then spent several minutes outlining the new reporting lines and the timeline for combining the two ticketing systems, striking a notably more confident tone once the conversation moved away from staffing. He closed by thanking the team for their patience during what he called ''a period of understandable uncertainty,'' and promised a further update within two weeks.', 'listening', 'C1', 'meeting')
  RETURNING id
)

WITH lp_passage_14 AS (
    VALUES ('Debriefing After a Client Escalation', 'After resolving a formal complaint from one of the firm''s largest retail clients, a customer service manager named Priya debriefed her team on how the case had been handled. The client had experienced repeated delivery failures over a six-week period and had threatened to move the account to a competitor. Priya explained that although a full refund and a goodwill credit had ultimately been issued, the client''s tone during the final call had remained noticeably reserved, and he had declined an invitation to renew the service contract early, saying he would ''wait and see how the next quarter goes.'' Priya cautioned the team against treating the case as closed, noting that the compensation had addressed the financial loss but might not have restored the client''s confidence in the service. She asked the account team to schedule a proactive check-in call within the next fortnight, rather than waiting for the client to raise further issues himself, and stressed that the account should be flagged internally as at risk until renewal was confirmed.', 'listening', 'C1', 'customer_service')
  RETURNING id
)

WITH lp_passage_15 AS (
    VALUES ('The Boardroom Standoff Over Q3 Numbers', 'The finance director cleared her throat before sliding the revised forecast across the table, conceding that the third quarter numbers were, in her words, a little softer than the market would like, though she was quick to add that softer was hardly the same as soft. The chief executive raised an eyebrow and asked whether a twelve percent shortfall against guidance still counted as merely soft. She replied that context mattered enormously, and that once you stripped out the currency swing and the delayed Rotterdam shipment, the underlying trend was, if anything, encouraging. The operations lead, who had said nothing for the first ten minutes, finally observed that they seemed to be discovering a remarkable number of one-off explanations lately, and wondered aloud whether the board might eventually start asking why these one-offs kept recurring with such regularity. A brief, uncomfortable silence followed. The chief executive then suggested, with the faintest trace of a smile, that perhaps it was time to stop calling them one-offs altogether, since investors would inevitably call them something else entirely: a pattern. The finance director promised a fuller variance analysis by Friday, and the meeting moved on, a little stiffly, to the next item on the agenda.', 'listening', 'C2', 'meeting')
  RETURNING id
)

WITH lp_passage_16 AS (
    VALUES ('Renegotiating Terms After the Due-Diligence Discovery', 'During the follow-up call on the acquisition terms, the lead negotiator for the buying side opened by saying she wanted to be upfront: the discovery of pending litigation in Brazil had, unsurprisingly, given her team pause. The seller''s representative countered that the litigation was, in his estimation, more nuisance than threat, and that folding it into the valuation discussion risked conflating a minor legal formality with a genuinely material business risk. She replied, diplomatically, that she wasn''t suggesting bad faith on anyone''s part, merely that thorough due diligence required her to treat every disclosed liability as material until proven otherwise, and that the current offer would need to reflect that caution. He asked, somewhat pointedly, whether this meant the price was simply being renegotiated outright. She said she would rather frame it as recalibrating the risk allocation, perhaps through an escrow holdback tied to the litigation''s resolution, rather than a straightforward reduction in the headline price. There was a pause on the line before he admitted that an escrow arrangement, while not his first preference, was at least a structure his board could live with, provided the release conditions were clearly defined. She agreed to circulate draft terms by the end of the week, and the call closed on notably warmer footing than it had begun.', 'listening', 'C2', 'negotiation')
  RETURNING id
)

WITH lp_passage_17 AS (
    VALUES ('Deciding How to Disclose the Data Incident', 'When the security team confirmed that a limited set of customer email addresses had been exposed during a routine software update, the general counsel convened an urgent call with the head of communications to decide how, and when, to inform affected customers. The general counsel argued that regulation gave the company a reasonable window to complete a full technical assessment before any public statement, and that rushing out a notice before the scope was fully understood risked causing needless alarm, or worse, having to issue a confusing correction later. The communications head countered, somewhat carefully, that customers tended to be far less forgiving of a delayed disclosure than an imperfect one, and that if the story broke first through a journalist rather than the company itself, the reputational cost would dwarf any embarrassment from an early, incomplete statement. There was a pause before the general counsel conceded that she wasn''t disputing the communications logic, only flagging that legal exposure was a real cost too, not merely a theoretical one. They ultimately agreed on a compromise: a brief holding statement within forty-eight hours acknowledging an incident under investigation, followed by full details once the technical review concluded, with legal reviewing every word of the initial statement before it went out.', 'listening', 'C2', 'meeting')
  RETURNING id
)
